# openabl kit provenance

abl_signed-SM8550.elf is NOT committed here - the PKGBUILD fetches it from the
OpenABL release, pinned by tag + sha512:

  release:  https://github.com/OpenHandheldCollective/OpenABL/releases/tag/v1.2
  asset:    openabl-v1.2.tar.gz (one folder per chipset)
  source:   openabl-v1.2-source.tar.xz on the same release (the GPL-3.0 corresponding source)
  tarball sha512: 41f8b0460bc5c2cbbec312911377e52c41eda7c4dcd365bc76486d81cf4da0e9
                  072df00edaf3afacb87565d856487d0aac3c8b5506d46b9ea6c901c319635492
  SM8550/abl_signed-SM8550.elf sha256 (release's own sidecar, re-checked in prepare()):
                  2a2808d12f2d494ddd2c39093bbffa90bc43402050be1a87baf6a4c37c830163

OpenABL is Qualcomm's ABL (edk2, BSD-3-Clause) with one patch and a menu
program (GPL-3.0-or-later), signed with the test key. One elf serves every
SM8550 board; OpenABL keys the artifact by SoC. It is not the ROCKNIX ABL v1.2
the kit shipped before (different bytes, same version label): OpenABL boots
Boot Loader Spec entries (loader/entries/*.conf, written by pocknix-bls-deploy),
not the /KERNEL Android boot image the ROCKNIX ABL loads. Until the boot image
is dropped the FAT carries both, so either ABL boots it.

The four kit files (README, flash_abl.sh.template, backup_abl.sh,
restore_backup_abl.sh) are VERBATIM copies from the ROCKNIX distribution repo,
packages/tools/openabl/sources/ (GPL-2.0 tree, next 13d241a7d3). The
PKGBUILD's %DEVICE% -> SM8550 substitution in flash_abl.sh reproduces their
package.mk. Vendored (4 tiny files, change rarely) rather than fetched or
synced; diff them against the ROCKNIX tree when advancing the sync pin.

Version coupling: the OpenABL version ROCKNIX pins lives in their
packages/tools/openabl/package.mk (PKG_VERSION). Ours (_ablver in the PKGBUILD)
should track it, and pocknix-abl-gate's MIN is the oldest OpenABL the boot
files are known to work with.

The kit's job is FIRST INSTALL from stock Android: the user copies openabl/ off
the SD's boot FAT to Android internal storage, runs backup_abl.sh (saves the
stock abl_a/abl_b next to the scripts - tell users to copy those .img backups
OFF the device; an install-to-internal later wipes userdata and the backup
with it), then flash_abl.sh, both as root. restore_backup_abl.sh is the road
back to a fully stock device.

Once a device runs pocknix, ABL UPDATES no longer need Android: pocknix-update-abl
(pocknix-bsp-common, reachable from Pocknix Tools) flashes this same elf onto
abl_a/abl_b from the running system. It is user-run only - no alpm hook, nothing
at boot or update time, unlike ROCKNIX's updateabl which their bootloader
update.sh calls on every OS update. Its backup goes to /flash/openabl with the
kit's own filenames and is never overwritten, so a stock backup made the Android
way survives and restore_backup_abl.sh keeps working. Backups made under the
old rocknix_abl folder are moved there (pocknix-refresh-abl-kit).
